own  by her narcoleptic husband  mr wheeler despite all the drama  revolving around them  never notices anything out of the  ordinary aside from a speech about  teamwork he gives to mike he spends  most of the time at home sleeping even  more mr  wheeler is a dud of a husband at the end  of season 2 we see mrs wheeler's jaw  drop when billy stops by while her  husband is passed out in his easy chair  by season 3 we realize how unfulfilled  mrs wheeler is in her marriage when she  makes plans to meet billy at a motel  even though she doesn't act on them mr  wheeler is an inattentive father and  husband but in reality it's tragic that  he's surrounded by people so full of  life while he  densely snores away yes these kids  are nerds choosing to hang out in the  hawkins av club instead of the football  field with basically no friends outside  their group  except their science teacher in fact  they may be the whole of the hawkins av  club  for their public school to have a ham  radio and an av club is certainly out of  the ordinary  but nerds are not they have some pretty  expensive hardware  their radios the radio shack trc 206  trc 214 and trc trc219  weren't cheap plus dustin has a headset  and later builds  cerebro a portable super powerful radio  at science camp  the boys certainly have free reign to  play with expensive toys  while their parents were likely more  than happy to encourage their scientific  endeavors the walkies and hundreds of  batteries they must go through in a year  would make a pretty expensive outlay  and this is before everything had usb  charging cables  murray bauman is the consummate  matchmaker in stranger things  when jonathan byers and nancy wheeler  come to him in season 2 for help the two  teenagers have  a bit too much vodka and wind up  sleeping over  bauman is surprised they're not together  and says they should just have sex  they protest and jonathan volunteers to  sleep on the pull out  that night the teenagers can't sleep and  when neither can hold out any longer  they take their friendship to the next  level  the next morning at breakfast bauman  asks with a wry smile  that was the pull out a kid watching  will think he's just joking about the  fact that jonathan didn't sleep on the  pull-out sofa bed  a grown-up however we'll see the double  entendre he makes with the pull-out sofa  and  a particular move that happens in the  bedroom  during interrogation the russians give  steve and robin a truth serum  the drug could be anything but based on  the reaction  at least in a grown-up's eye it's likely  a psychoactive drug  it's worth mentioning that mk ultra the  program that gave rise to 11's psychic  powers  was known in the real world to  experiment with hallucinogenics and  psychology primarily for interrogation  purposes  when steve and robin start laughing and  joking with each other  to kids they seem drunk but adults will  understand that they're tripping  they seem drunk why would they be drunk  after having a mind-blowing experience  watching back to the future on  hallucinogens steve lists it as one of  his favorite movies when he interviews  to work at a video rental store while  sitting outside the theater the lights  on the ceiling of the mall begin to send  off chasers for robin and steve  that lands them in the bathroom throwing  up and then having one of the greatest  heart to hearts in stranger things  history  considering that these two were dosed  with some strong psychoactive drugs  steve's quick recovery and ability to  drive a car are pretty astounding  there is no way to explain how it was  having only one phone in the house  younger people will never understand the  necessity of taking a phone call  standing in the kitchen  in front of anyone who wants to listen  because the phone was mounted on the  wall next to the kitchen  for the grown-ups who mostly remember it  the central shared phone brings back  conflicted memories yes there was  something nice about not always being  accessible  but it hindered mobility and privacy  sometimes that short cord was replaced  by a long one you could stretch into any  room in the house though it was easy to  figure out when it was a private call  and even if you were in another room you  could never guarantee the call was  private  like when mrs wheeler picks up the other  phone to listen in on mike's call with  l only to hear him lie about his  grandmother being ill  as an adult in the era of play dates  constant chauffeuring  and stranger danger it can be shocking  to recall a time when parents let their  kids go off on their own all day  yet the kids of stranger things bike  around by themselves from house to house  over what is likely miles of empty road  in season three of stranger things  lucas's 10 year old sister  erica is left completely unsupervised at  the mall  her parents don't even seem to notice  that she spends basically a day in a  russian bunker  in the 80s kids were mostly left to  their own devices they made friends on  their own  biked home by themselves and their  parents let them take care of themselves  most of the day  sure that lack of supervision allowed  will to be snatched by the demogorgon  leaving his brother in charge of  surveillance duty but it also empowered  the kids to figure out what was going on  and save him and eventually the town  without parental intrusion  the old world was very disconnected  compared to our present world which adds  a lot to the story in stranger things  only grown-ups will fully notice and  remember how disconnected it was  after all how many times is everybody  separated and looking for each other in  this show  if they had cell phones they could  easily call each other to figure out  where everyone  else is located yes the kids do that  with their walkies but as we see in  season 2 they're not always equipped  with these handy devices  in the modern connected world however  people are constantly cell phone  accessible  today many parents have gps trackers on  their kids cell phones  but in this series there is no easy way  to figure out where the children are  in season one we see how much time  hopper and company spend  researching to find out that hnl was  involved with  mkultra in season 3 when the group  doesn't know planck's constant  they have to get dustin to use his super  powerful radio to connect to his  brainiac girlfriend  susie in salt lake city because she is  the only person they know who  understands the mathematical formulation  with the internet however you can look  up plank's constant in half a second  planck's constant is 6.62607004  in season 3 of stranger things we see  the impact the star court mall has had  on the small town of hawkins indiana  before malls people walked through  downtown areas going to random shops and  in bigger cities department stores  but shopping was mostly about  independent shop owners and locally  owned stores  or sometimes mail order then malls  brought in chains owned by far-off  conglomerates and while everybody  protested the death of downtown  districts  the malls became a central point of  american culture  they started in big cities but quickly  spread to smaller towns  many adults have memories of the early  days when they would go to the mall and  spend a few  hours walking around shopping catching a  movie and eating at the food court  this type of shopping center dominated  for a few decades until the internet  came around  now malls are lamenting their demise at  the hands of e-retail  ironically buying local in-store or  online at downtown shops seems to have  made a great resurgence  kids today may never understand how for  a brief while  malls were king in season two of  stranger things  steve and nancy go to the home of barb's  parents to talk about her disappearance  the parents couldn't get it together to  cook so they ordered kfc for dinner  joe kiri who plays steve harrington  spouts ad worthy lines about the finger  licking good chicken and adults who grew  up with overt product placements would  likely take notice  brands and storylines were a constant  since tv evolved into a machine driven  solely by ad revenue they were  eventually replaced by more expensive  commercials followed by the service  subscription model  and another product placement an adult  might notice new coke  lucas sounds like a commercial as he  pontificates to his friends on the  quality of this soda  in 1985 new coke was an attempt to  revitalize the brand and lucas's  description sounds like perfect  advertising  coca-cola even reissued new coke as a  tie-in to the show  in that regard stranger things has used  an old marketing trick in a  tongue-in-cheek way to bring old-world  marketing to modern television without  pricey commercials  california may have lost some of its  luster given the growth of other cities  and the high cost of living but in the  80s it was the place to be  while chicago enjoyed popularity thanks  to john hughes and new york has always  had a strong presence many movies and tv  shows about adolescents in the 80s were  set in california  in many ways the trends found in  california trickled to the rest of the  country  as evidenced by lucas and dustin using  popular phrases around max  you're super smart and you're like  totally  tubular this was the era before social  media and online shopping  when the only way to know the trends was  to be in the epicenter  so to leave that world would almost be  like falling back in time  small towns could be a season a year or  more behind the trends  billy is infuriated by this though his  status as a guy from california gives  him  instant cachet in hawkins with that said  leaving california for some place else  would have hit harder in 1984 than  almost any other time in history  because of this a person can almost  understand billy's frustration  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your f